Reasoning:
- `transferFrom` is public. Its parameters `_from`, `_to`, and `_value` are untrusted. The function reads and writes `balances[_from]`, `balances[_to]`, and `allowed[_from][msg.sender]`. The checks are reversed: `fromBalance <= _value` and `allowance <= _value`. The branch requires `!overflowed`, where `overflowed = balances[_to] + _value > balances[_to]`, so the branch executes on wrapping addition. An external caller can choose untrusted `_value` large enough to wrap `balances[_to] + _value` and satisfy the reversed checks, reaching the writes to `balances` and `allowed`. No access control ties `_from` to `msg.sender`.
- `withdraw` is public and has no owner check. Any external `msg.sender` can call it. The function reads `this.balance` and calls `owner.transfer(etherBalance)`. There is no `require` or modifier restricting the caller to `owner`, so untrusted `msg.sender` reaches the sink.

